# Shell scripts are always LF, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A shell script is the one file type that cannot survive it: bash
# reads the trailing \r as part of the command and fails with
# `$'\r': command not found`, which names neither the file nor the real cause.
# The founder's working copy is on Windows and deploy.sh is run from Git Bash
# there, so this is not hypothetical.
#
# Deliberately narrow. A repo-wide `* text=auto` would renormalise 700-odd HTML
# and JSON files in one commit, which is a large diff for no benefit — every
# other format here is read by tools that do not care.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Git hooks have no extension but are run by bash through their shebang, so a
# CRLF checkout breaks every commit (`/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r'`). The hook
# adapters under scripts/ carry shebangs too and stay LF for the same reason.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
scripts/* text eol=lf
# The publishing canon, its tools and the client entrypoints are hashed byte for
# byte (readpack pins, the handoff's workflow fingerprints, the global-rule map).
# A CRLF checkout on Windows would make every one of those checks report drift,
# so they stay LF everywhere.
.dentcast/workflows/** text eol=lf
.dentcast/*.md text eol=lf
tools/*.py text eol=lf
tools/*.mjs text eol=lf
.cursor/** text eol=lf
.codex/** text eol=lf
.claude/** text eol=lf
.githooks/** text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md text eol=lf
AGENTS.md text eol=lf
